# d3-06 — product and model selection: boundaries, portability and closing judgement
projects-vs-chat|2|An associate keeps a Project for a piece of work that finished three months ago.|Archive it, since it no longer answers questions|Keep it, since the work may come back one day|Keep it and add the newer work into it as well|Delete the Project and all its documents entirely|A Project earns its place by answering current questions, and finished work belongs out of the way rather than competing with live material.|Hypothetical revival is how Projects accumulate, mixing new work in dilutes both, and deleting loses material that may still be needed.
scaling-decisions|2|A team's workflow works for one person and they want the whole team using it.|Find out what that person supplies that others will not|Give everyone the workflow and let them start using it|Have that person train each colleague individually|Wait until more people ask for it before sharing it|A working personal workflow usually carries unwritten context, and identifying it is what makes it survive the handover.|Handing it over unexamined exports the gaps, individual training does not scale, and waiting for demand delays a known benefit.
capability-matching|2|A team asks whether a stronger model will fix answers that are wrong because the data is old.|No — the data being old is not a capability problem|Yes — stronger models have more recent knowledge|Yes — stronger models are better at spotting staleness|No — but a stronger model would reduce the errors|Stale information produces stale answers regardless of how capable the model is, because the problem is the input rather than the reasoning.|Recency of training does not track the team's own data, spotting staleness is not having current facts, and capability does not substitute for correct data.
model-tiers|2|An associate asks how they should compare two tiers for their own particular task.|Run both on the same cases and compare the results|Read the published benchmarks for the two tiers|Ask colleagues which of the tiers they prefer using|Use the more expensive one, since it should be better|A tier's benefit is task-specific, so the only decisive comparison is on the cases the workflow actually meets.|Benchmarks measure other tasks, colleague preference is anecdote, and price is a proxy rather than a measurement.
surface-selection|2|A team wants an answer they can act on immediately and will not need again.|Ask directly and use the answer as it comes back|Build a repeatable workflow around the question|Document the question and answer for future use|Set the question up as a scheduled recurring task|A single, immediate need is served by asking, and any structure around it costs more than it can return.|A workflow, documentation and a schedule all invest in a repetition that is never going to happen.
feature-fit|2|A team needs the model to work through a derivation where the intermediate steps matter.|Use a setting that shows the working, not just the result|Ask for the answer and check it independently after|Ask for the answer and then ask how it was reached|Break the derivation into separate small questions|When the steps are what has to be checked, they have to be produced as part of the work rather than reconstructed afterwards.|Independent checking repeats the derivation, an after-the-fact explanation is a new output rather than a trace, and splitting loses the continuity.
cost-latency|2|An associate asks what it is that actually drives the cost of a single request.|How much text goes in and how much comes back out|How long the request takes to be answered fully|How difficult the question is to answer properly|How many times the request has been run before|Cost tracks the volume of text in and out, which is why long context and long answers are what move the bill.|Time, difficulty and history all feel like cost drivers but are not what is actually measured.
projects-vs-chat|2|A team wants new joiners to get consistent answers about their internal processes.|Give them a Project holding the process documents|Have each new joiner ask an experienced colleague|Send new joiners the documents to read themselves|Write a list of frequently asked questions for them|A Project answers questions in the joiner's own words from the authoritative documents, which is what makes the answers consistent.|Colleagues give varying answers, unread documents help nobody, and a fixed FAQ only covers the questions someone anticipated.
scaling-decisions|2|A team's costs will rise with volume and they want to know when to start worrying.|Work out the cost per item and project it forward|Wait until the monthly bill becomes noticeable|Set a monthly cap and stop when it is reached|Assume the cost will stay roughly where it is now|A per-item cost turns volume plans into a forecast, which is what lets the team see the problem before it arrives.|Waiting means reacting, a cap stops work rather than planning for it, and assuming stability ignores the growth they already expect.
capability-matching|2|A team's task is to sort tickets into twelve categories that are clearly documented.|A cheaper tier, tested against the documented categories|The strongest tier, since twelve categories is a lot|The strongest tier, since misclassification is costly|Whichever tier handles the highest volume fastest|Well-documented classification is within reach of cheaper tiers, and testing against the definitions is what confirms it for this data.|Category count is not difficulty, cost of error argues for verification rather than the top tier, and speed is not the fit question.
model-tiers|2|An associate reports that a cheaper tier gives correct but noticeably blander answers.|Decide whether the blandness actually matters here|Move back to the expensive tier, since quality dropped|Stay on the cheaper tier, since the answers are correct|Add an instruction asking for more engaging answers|Correctness and style are different requirements, and whether the style difference costs anything depends on who reads the output.|Both quick decisions assume an answer about the readers, and a style instruction treats a difference that may not need treating.
feature-fit|2|A team needs Claude to compare two long contracts clause by clause against each other.|Provide both contracts and ask for the comparison|Provide one contract and describe the other one|Compare a few clauses at a time across many chats|Summarise both contracts and compare the summaries|A clause-level comparison needs both texts present, since the differences live in the exact wording.|A description loses the wording, many small chats lose the overview, and summaries drop the very detail being compared.
surface-selection|2|A team wants Claude to draft replies inside the support tool their agents already use.|Integrate it there, since that is where the work happens|Have agents copy the ticket into a separate window|Have agents write drafts separately and paste them back|Train agents to switch between the two tools quickly|Putting the capability where the work already happens removes the copying that would otherwise be paid on every single ticket.|All three alternatives ask the agent to move data between tools on every ticket they handle.
projects-vs-chat|3|A Project answers well on its documents and confidently wrong outside of them.|Instruct it to say when a question falls outside the documents|Add more documents so fewer questions fall outside|Tell users only to ask questions the documents cover|Accept it, since the Project is for those documents|The failure is that out-of-scope questions get answered as if in scope, and telling it to flag them is what makes the boundary visible.|More documents move the boundary rather than marking it, instructing users relies on them knowing the contents, and accepting it leaves confident errors in place.
scaling-decisions|3|A team's workflow is being adopted by a second team with a slightly different goal.|Ask what their goal is before handing the workflow over|Hand it over, since the workflow is already proven|Rebuild it for the second team entirely from scratch|Have both teams share and maintain one workflow|A slightly different goal often means slightly different correctness, and finding out is what determines how much can be reused.|Handing over unexamined exports the first team's assumptions, rebuilding discards proven work, and shared ownership decides before the fit is known.
cost-latency|3|A team's requests are cheap individually and their monthly bill is nonetheless large.|Look at the volume rather than the per-request cost|Reduce the per-request cost by shortening prompts|Move to a cheaper tier to reduce the unit price|Accept it, since each request is already cheap|When unit cost is low and the total is high, volume is the driver, and that is where any reduction has to come from.|Prompt length and unit price both attack a cost that is already small, and per-request cheapness is what makes the total surprising.
capability-matching|3|A team's workflow fails specifically on inputs longer than about twenty pages.|Handle long inputs differently rather than changing tier|Move to a tier that handles longer inputs better|Reject inputs longer than twenty pages entirely|Truncate long inputs so they fit within the limit|A failure that appears at a length threshold is about how long inputs are processed, and restructuring that is the direct fix.|A tier change may raise the threshold without removing it, rejecting refuses valid work, and truncation silently drops content.
model-tiers|3|A team is told the newest tier is better and their workflow is carefully tuned already.|Retune before comparing, since tuning is tier-specific|Compare them as they are and take the better result|Switch to the newest tier, since it is an improvement|Stay put, since retuning would cost more than it gains|A prompt tuned for one tier can underperform on another, so comparing untuned against tuned measures the tuning rather than the tier.|An unfair comparison produces a wrong conclusion, switching on reputation skips the test, and assuming retuning is not worth it prejudges the gain.
surface-selection|3|An associate needs Claude to make the same twenty-step change across sixty files.|Use a surface that can act on the files directly|Do the twenty steps in a conversation for each file|Ask for instructions and apply them by hand to each|Do the first file carefully and copy the pattern across|Sixty repetitions of a mechanical change is exactly the case for something that operates on the files rather than describing the change.|All three alternatives put a person in the loop sixty times over.
feature-fit|3|A team needs a one-page visual summary that stakeholders can look at in a meeting.|Produce something rendered rather than described|Produce a written description of what it should show|Produce the numbers and let stakeholders visualise them|Produce a detailed report the meeting can work from|A visual summary has to be seen, so the deliverable is the rendered thing rather than an account of what it would contain.|A description, raw numbers and a report all leave the visualisation itself undone.
projects-vs-chat|3|A team's Project answers are shaped by an instruction that nobody remembers adding.|Review the instructions and remove what is not wanted|Add a counter-instruction to offset the unwanted effect|Rebuild the Project from scratch without instructions|Accept it, since the answers are still broadly useful|Instructions are a short, editable list, and reading them is the direct route to removing an effect nobody intended.|Counter-instructions stack rules that fight each other, rebuilding discards everything else, and accepting it leaves an unowned rule shaping answers.
cost-latency|3|A team's spend is fine and one workflow's latency is annoying its users daily.|Optimise the latency, since that is the live complaint|Optimise cost, since it is easier to measure and reduce|Optimise both, since they usually move together anyway|Accept the latency, since the spend is under control|Effort belongs on the dimension that is actually hurting, and here the users have already identified which one that is.|Measurability is not importance, cost and latency often trade rather than move together, and a healthy budget does not address the complaint.
capability-matching|3|A team wants to know whether a task needs a more capable model or a better prompt.|Try a better prompt on the current tier first|Try a stronger tier with the current prompt first|Try both of the changes together in order to save time|Assume it is the prompt, since prompts usually are|The prompt is the cheaper and faster variable to change, which makes it the sensible first test of two competing explanations.|Testing the tier first spends more to learn the same thing, changing both confounds the result, and assuming the answer skips the test.
scaling-decisions|3|A workflow will run in three countries and was designed around one country's rules.|Identify where the rules differ before extending it|Extend it and adjust wherever problems come up|Build a separate workflow for each of the countries|Extend it with a note that rules vary by country|Rule differences determine what the workflow must do differently, and mapping them is what shows how much can be shared.|Fixing problems as they appear means being wrong first, three separate workflows assume no overlap, and a note does not change behaviour.
model-tiers|3|An associate finds their task performs about the same on every tier they have tried.|Use the cheapest, since capability is not the limit|Use the strongest anyway, in case inputs get harder|Keep testing until a difference eventually appears|Conclude the measurement must be wrong somewhere|Equal performance means the task is not capability-limited, and the cheapest tier is then the rational choice.|Hypothetical future difficulty is not a current reason to pay, more testing repeats a settled result, and doubting the measurement without cause is unjustified.
surface-selection|3|A team wants Claude to act on a schedule and produce a report without being asked.|Use a surface that runs without a person triggering it|Ask someone to run the report each time it is due|Set a calendar reminder for whoever is on duty|Run it whenever the report is next needed by someone|The requirement is that it happens without human action, which only something self-triggering actually satisfies.|A person, a reminder and on-demand running all reintroduce the human step that was to be removed.
projects-vs-chat|3|A team's Project is slow compared with a plain conversation and members are complaining.|Reduce what the Project has to search through|Move the work back into plain conversations instead|Accept it, since Projects are inherently slower|Move to a faster tier to compensate for the delay|A Project's speed follows how much material it has to consider, so trimming it addresses the actual cause.|Abandoning the Project loses the grounding, "inherently slower" is not true of a well-curated Project, and a faster tier pays to search the same bloat.
capability-matching|4|A team's task needs both speed and careful reasoning and no tier offers both.|Split the task so each part gets what it needs|Choose the faster tier and accept weaker reasoning|Choose the stronger tier and accept a slower response|Wait for a tier that offers both characteristics|When one step needs speed and another needs care, separating them lets each be served instead of compromising both at once.|Either single choice sacrifices a real requirement, and waiting defers the work indefinitely.
projects-vs-chat|4|A consultant keeps one Project holding brand guidelines for all six of their clients.|One Project per client, so each answer sees only its own|Keep one Project and name the client in each question|Keep one Project and organise the files by client|Keep one Project but add instructions about the clients|A Project narrows what an answer draws on, and six clients' guidelines in one Project remove exactly that narrowing.|Naming the client relies on the asker, file organisation helps a browsing human rather than retrieval, and instructions do not reduce what is present.
scaling-decisions|4|A team must decide whether to build their own workflow or adopt a standard one.|Compare the fit gained against the cost of owning it|Build, since a custom workflow fits their task exactly|Adopt, since a standard workflow is maintained for them|Build now and move to the standard one later on|A custom build buys fit and takes on maintenance forever, and whether that trade is worth it depends on how much fit is actually gained.|Fit and maintenance are each one side of the trade, and planning to migrate later is a cost that usually never gets paid.
model-tiers|4|A team's rare errors are severe and occur on every tier at roughly the same rate.|Address it outside the tier choice, since tiers do not differ|Move to the strongest tier, since the errors are severe|Accept the errors, since no tier appears to avoid them|Test more tiers until one shows a lower error rate|When the error rate is flat across tiers, tier selection is not the lever, and the protection has to come from the process around it.|Buying capability that does not help wastes money, accepting severe errors is not an option, and more tier testing repeats a settled finding.
cost-latency|4|A team's users would accept a slower answer if it were more reliably correct.|Trade latency for accuracy, since the users have said so|Keep the current speed, since responsiveness matters|Improve both, since the trade should not be necessary|Ask the users again, since preferences change over time|The people who bear the latency have stated their preference, which is exactly the information the trade-off needed.|Defending speed overrides the users, improving both is not available when they genuinely trade, and re-asking a clear answer defers the decision.
feature-fit|4|A team must decide whether to summarise documents first or work from them in full.|Full where the detail decides, summary where the gist does|Always summarise first, since it is cheaper and faster|Always work in full, since summaries lose information|Summarise and keep the full text available as well|A summary is lossy in exactly the places a detailed question needs, so the task's dependence on specifics is what decides.|Absolute rules ignore the task, and supplying both makes them compete for the same attention.
surface-selection|4|A team's carefully built workflow only works when one particular person operates it.|Find what they do that the workflow does not capture|Have that person operate it as the standing arrangement|Simplify the workflow until anyone can operate it|Document the steps that person follows when running it|The gap between the workflow and one person's practice is unwritten knowledge, and identifying it is what can then be built in.|A standing arrangement makes them a permanent dependency, simplifying may remove what makes it work, and documenting steps can miss the judgement inside them.
capability-matching|4|A team's workflow is right 96% of the time and they must decide whether that is enough.|Ask what the four per cent costs and who bears it|Accept it, since 96% is already a high accuracy rate|Reject it, since 4% is too many errors to allow|Improve it until the rate reaches 99% or better|A percentage means nothing without the consequence attached, and who absorbs the failures is part of that consequence.|Accepting or rejecting on the number alone skips the consequence, and a target of 99% is chosen without a reason behind it.
projects-vs-chat|4|An associate asks the same questions of three reference documents several times a day.|A Project, since the documents recur across conversations|Attachments, since they guarantee the documents are seen|Pasting, since it puts the text directly in the prompt|One long conversation, so the context accumulates|Daily reuse of a stable set of documents is exactly the reuse test a Project is designed for.|Attaching and pasting repeat the same work many times a day, and one long conversation eventually loses its earliest content.
scaling-decisions|4|A pilot succeeded and the constraint on rollout is training rather than technology.|Treat training as the thing to design, not an afterthought|Roll out and let people learn it as they go along|Delay the rollout until the training material is perfect|Roll out only to people who need no training at all|When training is the binding constraint, it is the part of the rollout that determines success and deserves the design effort.|Learning as you go is how rollouts stall, waiting for perfect material never ships, and restricting to the already-capable never reaches the team.
feature-fit|4|A team needs output checked against a fixed list of rules on every single run.|Make the check a separate step rather than an instruction|Put the rules in the prompt and rely on it following them|Have a person check against the rules after each run|Put the rules in the Project's instructions instead|A rule set that must hold every time is a verification, and a separate deterministic check is what makes it reliable rather than merely likely.|Prompt instructions are followed most of the time rather than always, manual checking is expensive, and Project instructions share the same limitation.
cost-latency|4|A team's cheapest option would work if their inputs were cleaner, and cleaning costs money too.|Compare the cleaning cost against the tier saving|Clean the inputs, since clean data helps everything|Pay for the stronger tier and leave inputs as they are|Do neither, since both options cost about the same|Both routes reach the same result at different prices, so the comparison between them is the whole decision.|General enthusiasm for clean data is not this arithmetic, defaulting to the stronger tier assumes the cleaning costs more, and assuming parity avoids doing the sum.
model-tiers|4|A workflow will run for years and a tier it depends on may eventually be retired.|Keep the workflow portable so a tier change is possible|Choose the tier most likely to remain available longest|Ignore it, since retirement is announced well in advance|Build separate versions for two different tiers now|Portability is what makes any future tier change cheap, which is more durable than a prediction about which tier survives.|Predicting longevity is guesswork, advance notice still requires a workflow that can move, and maintaining two versions pays now for an uncertain event.
